            // In the reference, pot += 10; in mutant, pot += 11.
            // We can't directly observe the random outcome without inspecting state after each call.
            // Instead, we sum up expected pot after all calls if no win occurred.
            // However, the property we can assert: if pot > 0, then n * 10 <= pot (for reference)
            // But for mutant, pot > 0 may be n * 11.
            // The pot getter is public, so we can read c.pot().
        }

        // After the loop, pot value reflects the cumulative effect of all plays.
        // The invariant: pot must be a multiple of 10 if the contract is correct,
        // because each successful call adds exactly 10 wei when no win occurs.
        // If a win occurred and reset pot to 0, pot may be 0 or some smaller multiple.
        // The buggy version adds 11, so pot modulo 10 may be nonzero.
        uint256 currentPot = c.pot();
        assertTrue(currentPot % 10 == 0);
    }
}
